<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tenant-farmer">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tenant farmer</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tenant farmer is a farmer or farmworker who lives and works on land owned by a landlord, paying rent in a fixed share of the produce, in cash or in both, with rights that have varied widely: some could be evicted at will, others held contracts for a fixed number of years. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where tenants under Anglo-Norman law were almost all bound to the land as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villein">villeins</a>, free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a> grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, and by the 19th century about 90% of farmland was tenanted. In Ireland landlords held as much as 97% of the land in 1870, until the Land Acts let tenants buy their holdings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s tenant farmers, the husmenn, were about a quarter of its people in the mid-19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenant-farmer.jpg" alt="Tenant farmer" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Russell Lee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corbett_Infobox_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
